#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167995 :tutorial/content "Again, consider an autonomous system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=g(x(t),y(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfor which at a point $(x,y)$ in the phase plane, the tangent vector is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}^{\\prime } \\\\ {y}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}f ( x , y ) \\\\ g ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can now define two special sets of points in the phase space:\n\n- The **$x$-nullcline** is the set of points where $x'=0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the $x$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ {y}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nso the tangents to trajectories are *vertical*.\n\n- The **$y$-nullcline** is the set of points where $y'=0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}^{\\prime } \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nso the tangents to trajectories are *horizontal*.\n\nFor example, in the linear case, if the system is given as\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax(t)+by(t) \\\\ {y}^{′} &=cx(t)+dy(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthen the nullclines are the straight lines with equations\n\n$$\nax+by=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ncx+dy=0.\n$$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4abda1f-5bb9-5eb6-ae6f-f7c48b003f00" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14425 :tutorial/title "Nullclines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167996 :tutorial/content "A **phase portrait** is a diagram that shows the trajectories of a system along with their tangent directions.\n\nPhase portraits are usually generated with software. However, for simple linear systems, we can often sketch them by hand using the procedure below.\n\n- Start at a point $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Draw a short line segment in the direction indicated by the direction field at that point. To determine the direction, we can use the vector field (if given) or compute the tangent vector at $(x_0,y_0),$ as we did earlier.\n\n- Move a small step in the direction we just drew.\n\nRepeat Steps 2 and 3, taking small steps and adjusting the direction each time to match the direction field at the new point.\n\nAs we sketch, we can use *nullclines* to help determine where the trajectories must turn:\n\n- On the $x$-nullcline (where $x'=0$), the trajectory is vertical.\n\n- On the $y$-nullcline (where $y'=0$), the trajectory is horizontal.\n\nFinally, remember that trajectories do not form sharp corners: they bend smoothly as the tangent direction changes.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "54cb4976-e5e4-5f8d-beaf-ad953f0e39bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14427 :tutorial/title "Sketching Trajectories"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233913}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240789}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250131}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250188}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253534}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253536}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253539}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253545}]}
